# regressor.tkr — the compiler as its own full-stack regressor (0.3.1, regressor-principal
# design doc, C5). `[tests] regression` (teko.tkp) lists this file FIRST.
#
# IT BUILDS NOTHING, and that is the whole point of the CHANNEL layout (owner ruling 2026-07-26:
# *"tenho certeza absoluta que a grande maioria das regressões caberiam em um ou dois projetos no
# máximo"*). A variation in SOURCE needs a separate FILE inside one project; only a variation in
# BUILD CONFIGURATION earns a separate build. Every executable scenario this file used to carry
# therefore moved to the CHANNEL whose configuration it needs, and what is left here is the four
# DECLARATIVE R0 scenarios — living documentation of what the self-host build already proves.
#
# THE CHANNELS, and what each one costs on this host (measured, `teko: regressions … (N builds`):
#
#   * examples/regressions/bulk — DISSOLVED (owner ruling 2026-07-29, cargo/0.3.1-dissolver-bulk):
#     its 214 files were never distributed among the ten permitted regression channels this header
#     already commits to, so the whole project was retired rather than fixed. The cross-namespace
#     composition regressors it carried, and the slice of its language corpus that both builds and
#     runs correctly on the native backend today, moved into `own_native` below; the rest either
#     duplicated coverage this repo already has (the self-hosting build IS the language corpus) or
#     turned out to be currently BROKEN on the native backend — reported to the owner, not fixed
#     here, and not silently kept alive in a channel of its own.
#   * examples/regressions/own_native — the own-backend corpus (F2/F5/F9): ONE program, and one build
#     per TARGET, which is the only axis on it that varies. 3 builds on this host: the host project
#     itself, plus the lone rows for x86_64-windows and `host-os-arch`.
#   * examples/regressions/const_struct_ctor — a const struct built by a comptime call (degrau 1
#     da escada nativa). 1 build.
#   * examples/regressions/diagnostics — every checker-stage rejection, one source file per rejected
#     construct, ONE failing build reporting them all (the 0.3.1 all-diagnostics ruling). 3 builds:
#     that one, which now ALSO carries the two SOFT pre-walk rejections (`validate_type_decls` and
#     `check_modules` collect instead of stopping, so they no longer buy a build each); the HARD
#     SIGNATURE walk, which does stop at the first error and so cannot share; and the
#     unsupported-target boundary, which needs a build whose front end SUCCEEDS.
#   * examples/regressions/parse_diagnostics — the same for the PARSE stage, which aborts before the
#     checker runs and so cannot share the checker channel's build. 1 build.
#   * examples/regressions/crossmodule — the `package` (`.tkl`) dependency shape. 1 build.
#
# THE ARITHMETIC, stated so the next reader can audit it instead of re-deriving it. Two movements
# already landed and the count has to reflect BOTH, not one: um canal de alvo cruzado SAIU com o
# alvo que ele media (ruling do owner, .31), const_struct_ctor entrou (degrau 1 da escada nativa),
# e — o que uma versao anterior deste cabecalho esqueceu de subtrair — as passagens SOFT do pre-walk
# passaram a COLETAR, de modo que as quatro que compravam um build cada viraram uma dobrada na
# build que ja falhava.
#
# DEZ BUILDS, MEDIDOS — e este numero e o ALVO do dono ("9 builds por host, 10 se contar o
# proprio compilador como regressor"). O colapso em canais o alcancou.
#
# A medicao vale a lane FAIL-CLOSED (`regressor / all capabilities`), que e a unica que nao pula
# nada:
#
#     teko: regressions 9 run, 0 skipped, 3 failed (10 builds, 138.3s)
#
# Ler numero de build numa lane que PULA canais da menos e engana: a mesma arvore reportou "11
# builds, 2 skipped" num host sem as capacidades todas. Quem auditar tem de citar a lane.
#
# CORRECAO DE UMA CONTA MINHA, registrada de proposito em vez de apagada. Uma versao anterior deste
# cabecalho dizia TREZE — doze medidos na arvore anterior mais um derivado para o canal
# builtin_name_not_hijacked — e dizia, na mesma linha, que a primeira corrida verde confirmaria ou
# refutaria. Refutou: sao dez. O erro foi somar um build derivado a uma medicao feita com outro
# conjunto de canais, em vez de esperar a medicao inteira. A licao e a regra: numero de build se
# MEDE na lane fail-closed, nao se deriva por adicao.
#
# Cada um dos dez e uma configuracao DISTINTA de build ou um estagio DISTINTO de falha:
#
#     host (bulk) · host (own_native) · x86_64-windows · host-os-arch · const-struct
#     dependency shape · in-place shape · builtin-hijack · checker-fail · parse-fail
#     signature-prewalk-fail · unsupported-target-fail
#
# UPDATE (cargo/0.3.1-dissolver-bulk, 2026-07-29): `host (bulk)` in the list above no longer
# applies — `bulk` never actually fired in the measured-ten count in the first place (it was not,
# and had never been, wired into `teko.tkp`'s `[tests] regression` list; the entry was aspirational,
# not measured), and the project it named is now dissolved outright. The next fail-closed
# `teko: regressions … (N builds` run is the number to cite, not this list — MEASURE, do not derive
# by subtraction, the same rule the paragraph above this one already states.
#
# Um e removivel, e nomeia quem o remove:
#
#   1. o build do SIGNATURE walk dobra na build de checker-fail o dia em que essa passagem HARD
#      tambem coletar em vez de parar no primeiro erro — trabalho do all-diagnostics, nao deste
#      canal. As duas passagens SOFT ja dobraram; e por isso que diagnostics caiu de seis para tres.
#
# THERE IS NO INLINE CODE IN THIS FILE OR IN ANY `.tkr` (owner ruling: "está proibido inline code em
# tkr"). Every scenario names its source as a file a human can open, compile by hand and diff.
#
# THE BUILD NOBODY COULD SEE, found while counting: the snippet FOLD (`src/build/regr_group.tks`)
# builds its groups BEFORE the first scenario runs and adds their cost to the RUN total but never to
# any file's own totals — so a per-file audit summed to fifteen while the run reported seventeen, and
# the two missing builds were its. With the own-backend corpus a project, no row in the corpus is
# groupable any more (a project row carries no snippet), the fold plans nothing and builds nothing,
# and the per-file sum now equals the run total exactly. The fold's own reason to exist died with the
# backend axis: `regr_group_solo` has been unconditionally true since the `TEKO_BACKEND` selector was
# excised, and a solo group is precisely what the `(file, declared env)` cache already gives a
# `Given source` row for free — while ALSO serving the rows the fold refuses (an object check), which
# is the build it was costing. It is dormant, not deleted: that is a compiler change, not a channel
# one, and it is reported rather than taken here.
#
# THE F3 NULL-UNION MATRIX (regressor-principal-0.3.1.md §3.1) lives in the channels, not here. Two
# cells are HONEST GAPS pinned as compile-fail scenarios rather than silently dropped — a slice
# whose ELEMENT is itself a null-union (`[]T` where `T = X | null`) is REJECTED today (the parser
# reads `[](` as a FUNCTION type; spelled through an alias it reaches codegen's "optional/slice
# inner type not yet supported"), and an `Iface | null` (interface-in-union) construction is a
# checker-level KNOWN-STOP. The day support lands each pin fails LOUDLY, forcing a conscious
# promotion to a positive scenario. FFI `ptr<T> | null`/`uptr | null` (§3.1 "FFI") is NOT authored —
# there is no existing in-tree `ptr<T>`/`uptr` USAGE pattern to model a snippet against safely;
# flagged for the architect/owner, not guessed.
#
# UPDATE (cargo/0.3.1-dissolver-bulk, 2026-07-29): this paragraph used to say the matrix's shape ×
# operation × recursion cells were "a runnable member of `bulk`" — `bulk` is dissolved. Triaging its
# corpus while dissolving it found the matrix in WORSE shape than that sentence claimed: several
# cells that nominally "ran" on the native backend either hit the SAME already-pinned struct-
# literal-field-null-init stop `native_union_nested_known_stop` pins (reported there, not re-pinned)
# or produced a silently WRONG value (a nested `Op | null` field read back 0 instead of 42, a
# self-referential boxed list read back 0 instead of 42 — a genuine native-backend correctness gap,
# not merely an unimplemented one). Only the plain (non-nested, non-recursive) shapes — a `struct`
# and a `class` carrying a scalar `T | null` field, and a forwarded un-narrowed return — survived
# triage intact and moved to `examples/regressions/own_native` as part of that same dissolution.
#
# The root-project guard (`guard_no_project_build_at_root`) refuses a scenario here with no
# inline/explicit source — which is exactly why every scenario below is declarative.
Feature: R0 — the compiler is its own full-stack regressor (exe native, declarative).

  # The compiler is a native `binary` artifact, so R0 already IS the regressor of the "exe native"
  # artifact kind AND of the whole LANGUAGE. The curated regressors under examples/regressions cover
  # only what R0 structurally CANNOT exercise (other artifact kinds, targets, FFI) — never language
  # features (those live in the channels above + the checker `#test` suite).

  Scenario: the compiler compiles the whole language
    # verified-by: the seed builds gen1 from the umbrella sources (teko . -o bin) — the self-host build.


  Scenario: the compiler passes its own gate
    # verified-by: teko test . exits 0 (600+ checker/build #tests + the D4 coverage floors).


  Scenario: the compiler reaches a byte-identical fixpoint
    # verified-by: gen1 == gen2 byte-identical (the seed-compiled and self-compiled compilers agree),
    # NEVER gen3 — the two-generation fixpoint is the release invariant.


  Scenario: the emitted code agrees across targets
    # verified-by: the own_native channel compiles ONE program for the host and for x86_64-windows, and every target asserts the same exit —
    # plus `Then object well-formed` on each cross target. The arc own==C that this scenario also
    # asserted MORREU with the emissor de C (ruling do owner 2026-07-26): there is no second backend
    # left to differ against.


Feature: native process entry — teko::env::args() reaches the own-backend's argc/argv (0.3.1 native-args fix)

  # `teko::env::args()` is a term USED IN PRODUCT SOURCE (`src/build/project.tks`:
  # `allow_undef_selected()` reads it to resolve a build flag; `self_exe_path` resolves argv[0]
  # against the launch cwd), so this regression lives HERE, at the project root, never in a channel
  # (owner ruling 2026-07-29 — a regression of a term the product source itself uses is regressed
  # strictly in `/regressor.tkr`).
  #
  # The synthesized own-backend `main` used to take NO parameters and never called the runtime's
  # `tk_set_args`, so `teko::env::args()` (`tk_rt_args`) always observed the runtime's
  # zero-initialized globals — an EMPTY slice, no matter what the process was actually invoked
  # with. This is NOT a corner case reserved for user programs: it sits on self-hosting's own
  # critical path — a `teko` binary built BY the own backend could not read its own command-line
  # options, `allow_undef_selected()` included.
  #
  # Guard-compliant: this scenario names an EXPLICIT `Given source` file (`cases/`), the only shape
  # `guard_no_project_build_at_root` allows at the project root (R0's own header, above).
  Scenario: argv reaches the own-backend's synthesized process entry
    Given source = "cases/native_entry_argv.tks"
    Given args = ["alpha", "beta", "gamma"]
    When built and run
    Then exit = 4

Feature: builtin-qualifier validation — a builtin call is resolved by NAME and QUALIFIER, not name alone (0.3.1.0, owner ruling 2026-07-29)

  # `teko::str::concat` is a term USED IN PRODUCT SOURCE 495+ times (`builtin_fn`,
  # `src/checker/scope.tks`, matches a call by its LAST path segment only), so a regression of its
  # dispatch mechanism lives HERE, at the project root, never in a channel (the SAME 2026-07-29
  # ruling as the `teko::env::args()` Feature above).
  #
  # Measured and documented in docs/memory/colisao-use-str-0.3.1.md §7: before this fix, a call
  # through ANY qualifier — invented, a real namespace missing its `teko::` root, or a `use`-
  # declared alias — resolved to the SAME builtin as the canonical `teko::X::name(...)` spelling,
  # with NO checker diagnostic; only an accident of codegen (its own gate only knows how to lower a
  # bare or `teko::`-rooted callee) turned it into a `cc`/`ld` failure downstream. `builtin_qualifier_ok`
  # (`src/checker/typer.tks`) closes that gap at the checker, honestly, before codegen ever runs.
  Scenario: a builtin call through an invented namespace is rejected, not silently miscompiled
    Given source = "cases/qualifier_bogus_namespace_rejected.tks"
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "unknown function: bogus_namespace::str — `bogus_namespace` is not a recognized namespace"

  # `use teko::str as tstr` is a checker no-op for value-level references today (`UseDecl` passes
  # through verbatim) — an alias must NOT make a builtin resolvable under its short name until that
  # separate, larger increment lands. Silently accepting it would be the exact hazard the owner
  # asked to have tested (docs/memory/colisao-use-str-0.3.1.md's opening probe).
  Scenario: a builtin call through a `use`-declared alias is rejected, not silently resolved
    Given source = "cases/qualifier_alias_not_builtin_rejected.tks"
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "unknown function: tstr::concat — `tstr` is not a recognized namespace"

