    And stderr pattern = "teko: deliberate panic: division by zero"

Feature: R0 fat-predicate agreement — a type ALIAS of `str`/`[]T` sizes and stores identically to `str`/`[]T` itself (0.3.1.0 raiz A, crumb R0, 2026-07-29)

  # `type S = <T>` (a type alias) and `str` are terms USED IN PRODUCT SOURCE throughout this
  # compiler (`checker::TypeTable` alone is `type TypeTable = []TypeReg`), so this regression lives
  # HERE, at the project root, never in a channel — the SAME ruling `native process entry` (above)
  # already cites (owner ruling 2026-07-29: a regression of a term the product source itself uses
  # is regressed strictly in `/regressor.tkr`).
  #
  # Measured LIVE on this vagão before the fix (raiz-comum memo §7-quater.3-bis): the layout
  # predicate (`typeexpr_is_fat`, syntactic — asked the ANNOTATION before any resolution) and the
  # store predicate (`is_fat_type`, semantic — asked the field VALUE's resolved type) disagreed on
  # the SAME field the moment that field's annotation was a type ALIAS of `str`. `field_layout_size`
  # reserved 8 bytes for the aliased field, so its sibling field landed exactly where the fat
  # field's own length half needed to go — a silent misread before crumb R3's two-half store
  # landed, a silent CORRUPTION of the sibling after it (R3 without R0).
  Scenario: alias_fat_field (own-native)
    Given source = "cases/alias_fat_field.tks"
    When built and run
    Then exit = 0

  Scenario: alias_fat_field (C route)
    Given source = "cases/alias_fat_field.tks"
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When built and run
    Then exit = 0

Feature: null-union member comparison — `a == null`/`a != null` (and a numeric-literal sibling) over an explicit `T | null` variant compile and give the right value on both routes (0.3.1.0, cargo/0.3.1.0-null-adopt)

  # `a != null` over an EXPLICIT `i32 | null` variant (owner's own literal example) compiled on
  # NEITHER route: the checker wrongly ACCEPTED it (`adopt_binop_operands` retyped the adopted bare
  # `null` to the WHOLE union instead of narrowing it to `Null`, `retype_literal`'s own correct arm),
  # so codegen honestly rejected the mistyped bare `null` downstream — "a bare `null` needs a known
  # optional type from context". A LANGUAGE gap (fails on both routes, no oracle to measure a fix
  # against), not a route-specific bug.
  #
  # THE OWN-NATIVE HALF MOVED, cargo/0.3.1-dissolver-bulk (2026-07-29): the two obstacles that used
  # to keep it here are both gone. `examples/regressions/bulk` (the channel the R0 header above
  # used to name for the F3 null-union matrix) is DISSOLVED — never distributed among the ten
  # permitted regression channels, owner ruling 2026-07-29 — so it is no longer a destination to
  # wait on. And `examples/regressions/own_native/src/corpus.tks`'s exit-code budget, full at 56
  # then, now runs past it (`f_variant_member_compare`, code 60). The default-backend half now
  # lives there, off a build that already exists. Only the C-ROUTE half stays here: this project's
  # own charter (`own_native.tkp`'s header) is native-only — "no scenario here differs against a
  # second backend" — so a `TEKO_BACKEND=c` row has no home there, and this ONE row is cheaper than
  # the two this Feature used to cost (the own-native half now shares `own_native`'s build; only
  # the C route still needs its own throwaway build).
  Scenario: variant_member_compare (C route)
    Given source = "cases/variant_member_compare.tks"
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When built and run
    Then exit = 0

Feature: teko::text byte-view escape hatches — `bytes_of_str`/`str_from_utf8` moved off `teko::str::` (0.3.1.0, owner ruling 2026-07-29)

  # `bytes_of_str`/`str_from_utf8` are terms USED IN PRODUCT SOURCE — `src/numeric/bigint/bigint.tks`
  # and `src/numeric/dec/dec.tks` call the QUALIFIED `teko::text::` spelling directly; `src/regex/
  # regex.tks`, `src/io/stream.tks`, `src/encoding/csv/csv.tks`, `src/encoding/base64/base64.tks`,
  # `src/encoding/url/url.tks`, `src/encoding/json/json.tks`, `src/iter/str_iter.tks` and
  # `src/iter/byte_iter.tks` call the BARE spelling (20+ call sites) — so a regression of their
  # dispatch lives HERE, at the project root, never in a channel (the SAME ruling the
  # `builtin-qualifier validation` and `native process entry` Features above already cite).
  #
  # MEASURED, not assumed. A prior agent on this lane flagged a possible infinite recursion: a Teko
  # WRAPPER named `teko::text::bytes_of_str` calling the bare builtin of the SAME name would recurse
  # forever (same-name-same-namespace wins in `lookup_call`), so no such wrapper was written. That
  # reasoning is REFUTED by measurement, not merely by reading: no such wrapper exists anywhere in
  # this tree — `src/text/text.tks` documents `bytes_of_str` in a COMMENT only (its real definition
  # is "Registered as a builtin … this comment is the canonical documentation") and never defines it
  # as a Teko function — so the recursion could never have fired. `builtin_fn` (`scope.tks`) matches
  # both names by LAST segment and `builtin_qualifier_ok` (`typer.tks`) accepts a callee literally
  # rooted at `teko::` at ANY depth, so `teko::text::bytes_of_str(s)` / `teko::text::str_from_utf8
  # (bytes)` resolve to the injected builtin TODAY, with NO wrapper, on the C route — confirmed by
  # the emitted C calling `tk_bytes_of_str`/`tk_rt_str_from_utf8` directly. The doc-comment at
  # `scope.tks:498-499` claiming this canonical spelling already needed no code change, only this
  # proof.
  Scenario: teko::text byte-view round-trip on a multi-byte string (C route)
    Given source = "cases/text_byteview_roundtrip.tks"
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When built and run
    Then exit = 0

  # (0.3.1.0 degrau 21) The own-native gap the sibling Feature above USED TO carry as a KNOWN-STOP
  # is CLOSED: `native_builtin_symbol` now resolves `bytes_of_str` (`builtin_bytes_of_str_symbol`,
  # the SAME `_len` out-parameter molde `builtin_str_of_bytes_symbol` already uses for its own
  # reverse direction), and `str_from_utf8`'s `str | error` result — a fat payload wrapped in a
  # variant, neither bare-fat nor scalar — gets its own bool-branch + variant-wrapper construction
  # (`lower_str_from_utf8_call`), mirroring degrau 15's `last_index_of`. Checked by VALUE, not
  # merely by compiling: this row shares the EXACT source the C-route scenario above runs, so a
  # divergence between the two routes (a wrong length, a swapped byte, a lost error message) shows
  # up as a wrong exit here, never a silent pass.
  Scenario: teko::text byte-view round-trip on a multi-byte string (own-native)
    Given source = "cases/text_byteview_roundtrip.tks"
    When built and run
    Then exit = 0

